WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.115
Cryptographic verification establishes
that the protected token

2
00:00:03.115 --> 00:00:05.916
content matches the expected key and
algorithm.

3
00:00:05.916 --> 00:00:09.839
Use a maintained JOSE or JWT library for
this operation.

4
00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:12.080
Application code should configure policy
and

5
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.041
handle results, not implement signature

6
00:00:14.041 --> 00:00:16.656
parsing, cryptographic primitives, or
compatibility workarounds.

7
00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:19.831
Symmetric protection, such as an HMAC,
uses a

8
00:00:19.831 --> 00:00:23.380
shared secret to create and verify the
authentication code.

9
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.995
Every verifier holding that secret can
also

10
00:00:25.995 --> 00:00:28.890
create tokens, so distribution expands
signing authority.

11
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.131
Use strong generated key material and

12
00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:33.652
restricted custody, not a memorable
password.

13
00:00:33.652 --> 00:00:35.894
Asymmetric signing separates a private
signing

14
00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:37.948
key from public verification keys.

15
00:00:37.948 --> 00:00:42.244
APIs can verify signatures without
receiving the authority to issue tokens.

16
00:00:42.244 --> 00:00:45.045
The verifier decides which algorithms are
acceptable.

17
00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:48.594
The token does not get to expand that
policy.

18
00:00:48.594 --> 00:00:52.797
Configure a narrow allowlist appropriate
to the issuer and key material,

19
00:00:52.797 --> 00:00:57.092
and ensure the selected key type matches
the expected algorithm family.

20
00:00:57.092 --> 00:00:59.334
Do not mix symmetric and asymmetric

21
00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:01.762
models simply to accommodate whatever
arrives.

22
00:01:01.762 --> 00:01:05.684
Reject unsigned tokens at a boundary that
requires authenticated access.

23
00:01:05.684 --> 00:01:08.859
The JOSE header is untrusted input before
verification.

24
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:12.408
Its algorithm, key identifier, and type
fields can guide

25
00:01:12.408 --> 00:01:16.050
controlled processing, but they do not
independently establish trust.

26
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.665
A library may inspect those fields to

27
00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:21.466
select an allowed operation and candidate
key.

28
00:01:21.466 --> 00:01:23.708
The server still owns the accepted

29
00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:26.696
algorithm set, key source, and token
profile.

30
00:01:26.696 --> 00:01:28.564
Reject unsupported critical header
requirements

31
00:01:28.564 --> 00:01:30.618
rather than silently ignoring them.

32
00:01:30.618 --> 00:01:34.540
The conceptual example allows one
algorithm for a configured issuer.

33
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.996
Its exact API syntax varies by library,
and algorithm

34
00:01:37.996 --> 00:01:41.544
selection is only one part of the full
verifier.

35
00:01:41.544 --> 00:01:43.879
Review library documentation and secure
defaults,

36
00:01:43.879 --> 00:01:46.587
keep dependencies maintained, and test
rejection of

37
00:01:46.587 --> 00:01:49.015
disallowed algorithms and incompatible key
types.

38
00:01:49.015 --> 00:01:52.844
A successful signature check must flow
into claim validation; it

39
00:01:52.844 --> 00:01:57.140
must never become an automatic permission
to enter every protected route.

